
{"id":"36bb9a51-03a2-4e83-bf99-354801f30e1c","object":"chat.completion","created":1784826205,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\\n    required = set(payload['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_by_id}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n    alive = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    total_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required:\\n            total_counts[sym] = total_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n\\n    def get_subtree(node_id):\\n        sub = set()\\n        stack = [node_id]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur not in alive:\\n                continue\\n            sub.add(cur)\\n            for ch in children.get(cur, []):\\n                if ch in alive:\\n                    stack.append(ch)\\n        return sub\\n\\n    def sub_counts(node_id):\\n        sub = get_subtree(node_id)\\n        cnt = {}\\n        for sid in sub:\\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\\n            if sym in required:\\n                cnt[sym] = cnt.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n        return cnt\\n\\n    changed = True\\n    while changed:\\n        changed = False\\n        removable = [nid for nid in node_by_id if node_by_id[nid]['removable'] and nid in alive]\\n        for nid in removable:\\n            if nid not in alive:\\n                continue\\n            sc = sub_counts(nid)\\n            safe = True\\n            for sym, cnt in sc.items():\\n                if total_counts[sym] - cnt <= 0:\\n                    safe = False\\n                    break\\n            if safe:\\n                sub = get_subtree(nid)\\n                for sym, cnt in sc.items():\\n                    total_counts[sym] -= cnt\\n                alive -= sub\\n                changed = True\\n                break\\n    kept = sorted(alive)\\n    return {'kept': kept}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem. The task is to implement a function `solve(case)` that takes a case payload (a dict with \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\") and returns a JSON-serializable object. The goal: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" – reduce a parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\n\nFrom the fixture, we have a tree of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The required_symbols list indicates which symbols must be preserved? Possibly we need to remove removable nodes while ensuring that the remaining tree still contains all required symbols. But there is also \"utility\" values – maybe we need to maximize sum of utilities? Or we need to output the set of nodes to keep? The spec says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" That sounds like we need to produce a reduced tree (subset of nodes) that satisfies some constraints.\n\nGiven the limited info, likely we need to return the IDs of the nodes that should be kept (or removed) such that required_symbols are present, and maybe we can only remove nodes that are marked removable. Also \"grammar-valid\" might refer to the tree structure: after removal, the tree must still be valid (i.e., each node's parent must exist, etc.) Possibly we can only remove leaf nodes? Or we can remove internal nodes and connect children to grandparent? Not sure.\n\nBut the problem says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". Maybe we can choose to delete a node (if removable) or replace it with something? The fixture shows each node has a symbol, and required_symbols is a list. Perhaps we need to return a set of nodes to delete such that the remaining nodes still include all required_symbols. But there is also utility – maybe we want to maximize total utility of kept nodes? Or minimize? Since \"reduce\" suggests we want to remove as many as possible? But we have to preserve interestingness oracle, which might be that required_symbols are present.\n\nLet's look at the fixture cases. They all have the same structure: root (symbol root), n1 (s1), n2 (s2), n3 (s0), n4 (s1 under n2), n5 (s2 under n3), n6 (s0 under n1). Some nodes are removable: n2, n4, n6. required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So we must keep at least one node with symbol \"root\" and at least one with symbol \"s1\". The root is not removable. s1 appears in n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable). So we can remove n4 if we still have n1. But we also have other symbols like s2 and s0 that are not required. So we could potentially remove all removable nodes as long as required symbols are still present. But we need to consider tree validity: if we remove a node, its children must be either removed or reattached? The problem mentions \"replacement candidates\" – maybe we can replace a node with one of its children? For example, remove n2 and replace it with its child n4? That would change the tree structure.\n\nGiven the depth and parent info, maybe we need to output the final tree after reduction. The \"registered parse tree\" suggests we have a parse tree that is registered (maybe has unique IDs). The goal is to produce a reduced version that is still grammar-valid (i.e., it corresponds to some parse according to the grammar) and preserves interestingness (i.e., contains required symbols). The grammar might be implicit from the symbols. The \"node reduction\" could mean we can delete subtrees or merge nodes. But without more info, we need to make reasonable assumptions.\n\nPerhaps we only need to return the IDs of nodes to keep. Looking at typical coding challenge, often the output is a list of node IDs that remain after reduction, or a tree structure.\n\nGiven the strict output format (single JSON object with \"implementation\" key containing Python source), and the function `solve(case)` must return a JSON-serializable object. So we need to implement something that produces the result for each case.\n\nLet's think about what the \"correct\" answer might be for the given fixtures. I'll examine the first case:\n\nNodes:\nroot: removable false, utility 1.0\nn1: parent root, symbol s1, removable false, utility 0.339\nn2: parent root, symbol s2, removable true, utility 0.798\nn3: parent root, symbol s0, removable false, utility 0.124\nn4: parent n2, symbol s1, removable true, utility 0.428\nn5: parent n3, symbol s2, removable false, utility 0.428\nn6: parent n1, symbol s0, removable true, utility 0.721\n\nRequired: root, s1.\n\nWe must keep root. For s1, we have n1 and n4. n1 is not removable, so we keep n1. n4 is removable; we could keep it or not. Since it's removable and we already have s1 from n1, we can remove n4. Also n2 is removable, and its only child is n4 (which we might remove). If we remove n2, n4 becomes orphaned? But n4's parent is n2; if n2 is removed, n4 must also be removed or reattached. Typically in tree reduction, if you delete a node, you delete its subtree. So removing n2 would also remove n4. That's fine.\n\nn3 is not removable, so we keep it. n5 is not removable, keep it. n6 is removable, its parent n1 is kept, but n6 is removable; we can remove it.\n\nThus we can remove n2, n4, n6. Kept nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. That yields a tree: root -> n1, root -> n3, n3 -> n5. No s2 required, so fine.\n\nBut what about utility? If we want to maximize total utility, we would keep all nodes (since utilities are positive). But \"reduce\" suggests we want to remove some. Perhaps we want to minimize total utility kept? Or we want to maximize sum of utilities of removed nodes? Or we need to consider \"interestingness oracle\" – maybe we must ensure that after removal, the tree still satisfies some property like having all required symbols, and we want to maximize the total utility of removed nodes (i.e., remove high-utility nodes if possible)? That seems odd.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the goal is to find the minimal set of nodes to delete such that the remaining tree still contains all required symbols. That would be a classic \"node deletion\" problem. But then we could delete all removable nodes except those needed to satisfy required symbols. In this case, we need root and s1. s1 is already satisfied by n1 (non-removable), so we can delete all other removable nodes: n2, n4, n6. That set is minimal.\n\nBut the problem mentions \"replacement candidates\" – maybe we can also replace a node with one of its children to preserve structure? For example, if we remove n2, we could replace it with n4 (since n2 has symbol s2, n4 has s1, but that would change the symbol? Not allowed if grammar-valid? Hmm.\n\nPerhaps the \"interestingness oracle\" is the set of required symbols that must appear in the reduced tree. And \"grammar-valid\" means the tree must be a valid parse tree according to some grammar, which might impose constraints on parent-child symbol pairs. For example, maybe only certain symbols can be children of others. The fixture doesn't provide grammar, so we must infer from the data? Or maybe the grammar is implicit: the tree is already grammar-valid, and we can only remove nodes that are removable, but we cannot change parent-child relationships unless we replace a node with its child (i.e., \"replacement\" is essentially pruning a node and making its children direct children of the grandparent). That is a common tree reduction operation.\n\nIn that case, for node n2 (removable), we could \"replace\" n2 by making n4 a direct child of root. But then n4's parent would be root, not n2. That would still keep the required s1 (through n4 or n1). But n4 is removable, so we could also remove n4 later. However, the replacement might allow us to remove n2 while keeping its child, which might be beneficial if that child is required or has high utility.\n\nBut the problem says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". So we have a choice: delete a node (removing it and its subtree) or replace a node (promoting its children). The goal is to reduce the tree while preserving interestingness (required symbols present).\n\nGiven the ambiguity, we need to produce a plausible solution. Since the problem is from \"effectslice\" and \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\", maybe the intended solution is to remove all removable nodes that are not required for the interestingness oracle. The required symbols list specifies symbols that must appear at least once in the reduced tree. So we can delete any removable node as long as its symbol is not the only occurrence of a required symbol in the tree.\n\nBut we also must consider tree validity: after deletion, the tree must remain a tree (i.e., no dangling children). If we delete a node, its children are also deleted (since they become unreachable). So deletion of a node implies deletion of its entire subtree. Therefore, we can only delete a node if the deletion of its subtree does not remove the last occurrence of any required symbol.\n\nAdditionally, we might have replacement operation: replace a node with its children (i.e., remove the node but attach its children to its parent). This is similar to deleting the node but preserving its children. That operation might allow us to remove a node even if its subtree contains required symbols, by keeping the children.\n\nSo the problem could be: given a tree where each node has a symbol and a removable flag, and a set of required symbols, we can either delete a node (if removable) which removes it and its subtree, or replace a node (if removable) which removes it but reattaches its children to its parent. The goal is to apply a sequence of such operations to reduce the tree (perhaps minimize number of nodes or sum of utilities of removed nodes?) while ensuring all required symbols still appear. The output should be the resulting tree (set of nodes that remain) with possibly modified parent pointers after replacements.\n\nThe function `solve(case)` should return the reduced tree in some JSON-serializable form. The fixture doesn't specify output shape, but we can return a list of node IDs that are kept, or the full node list with updated parent relations.\n\nGiven the strict output requirement (the JSON object with implementation key), we must write Python code that processes the case payload. We'll need to parse nodes, build adjacency, determine which nodes are removable, and then apply reduction. I'll assume we can only delete nodes that are removable, and after deletion, children are lost. So we need to find a maximal set of nodes to delete such that required symbols are still present. That is a constraint satisfaction: we can delete a node if its subtree does not contain the only occurrence of any required symbol. But because deletions can affect multiple occurrences, we need to check globally.\n\nA simpler approach: Since the tree is small (7 nodes), we could try all subsets of removable nodes to delete, ensure remaining tree (excluding deleted nodes and any nodes that become disconnected) contains all required symbols, and then choose the one that minimizes total utility of kept nodes (or maximizes removed utility). But we don't know the objective. The problem says \"bounded objective: Reduce ... while preserving ...\". So we need to reduce, meaning we want to remove as many nodes as possible? Or minimize the size? Probably minimize the number of nodes, but with constraint that required symbols are present.\n\nLet's test with the first case: if we delete n2, n4, n6, we keep 4 nodes (root, n1, n3, n5). Can we delete more? n1 is not removable, n3 not removable, root not removable, so no. So that's minimal? But we could also replace n2 with n4? That would remove n2 but keep n4 (so n4's parent becomes root). Then we could also delete n4? But n4 is removable, and if we delete n4, we lose s1, but we still have n1 with s1. So we could delete both n2 and n4? If we replace n2, n4 becomes child of root; then we could delete n4, which would remove its subtree (none). So final nodes: root, n1, n3, n5, and n6? n6 is removable, we could delete n6. That yields same set of 4 nodes. So no improvement.\n\nWhat about deleting n1? n1 is not removable, so no.\n\nThus the minimal size seems 4.\n\nWe need to find if removing n3 is possible? n3 is not removable. So we can't.\n\nSo a simple greedy approach: iterate removable nodes, and if removing that node (and its subtree) still leaves all required symbols in the rest of the tree, then remove it. But order matters; we might need to consider that removing a node could make another node's removal viable later. That's okay.\n\nAlternatively, we could compute the total set of removable nodes that can be removed without losing required symbols. For each required symbol, we need at least one occurrence that is not deleted. So we can only delete a node if for every required symbol, there exists at least one occurrence outside that node's subtree (or in another branch). So we can delete all removable nodes whose subtree does not contain the only remaining occurrence of any required symbol. This is like we can delete any removable node whose deletion does not cause a required symbol to disappear.\n\nSo algorithm:\n- Identify all nodes with symbol in required_symbols.\n- For each required symbol, keep a count of how many nodes have that symbol. Initially counts.\n- For each removable node, consider deleting it and its entire subtree. For each required symbol, subtract the count within that subtree from the global count. If any count becomes zero, then deletion is not allowed. If allowed, we mark it for deletion, update global counts (by removing those nodes), and continue.\n\nBut care: after deleting some nodes, counts change. So we need to iterate until no more deletions possible.\n\nLet's test on first case:\n- Required: root, s1.\n- Counts: root:1 (only root), s1:2 (n1, n4).\n- Consider removable nodes: n2 (symbol s2, subtree contains n4 with s1). If delete n2, s1 count goes to 1 (still ok), root count unchanged (1). So deletion allowed. Also n4 (subtree just itself, s1 count would go to 1, ok). n6 (subtree just itself, symbol s0, not required, so fine). So we could delete all three. After deleting n2, n4, n6, counts: root:1, s1:1 (n1). So feasible. Result: keep root, n1, n3, n5.\n\nNow check if we can delete n5? n5 is not removable. So done.\n\nNow, could we delete n2 and n4? After deleting n2, n4 is gone anyway. So it's same.\n\nNow, what about the case where deleting a removable node would remove the only occurrence of a required symbol? For example, if required_symbols were [\"root\",\"s2\"], then root is fine, s2 appears in n2 and n5. n2 is removable, n5 not removable. Deleting n2 would remove its own s2, but n5 remains, so allowed. If required_symbols were [\"root\",\"s0\"], then s0 appears in n3 (non-removable) and n6 (removable). Deleting n6 would keep n3 so allowed. Deleting n3 not allowed because not removable.\n\nSo this greedy approach works.\n\nBut the problem mentions \"replacement candidates\". Why would we need replacement if deletion suffices? Possibly because the tree must remain connected and valid. For example, if we delete a node that has children, those children are lost. But if that deleted node is the only path to some required symbol, we cannot delete it. However, we could replace it (promote its children) to keep the children while removing the node. So replacement gives more flexibility: we can remove a node but keep its children.\n\nIn the above, we never needed replacement because the removable nodes with required symbols in their subtrees (like n2) had other occurrences of those required symbols outside. But consider a case where a required symbol appears only in the subtree of a removable node. Then we cannot delete that node, but we could replace it (promote its children) to preserve the subtree while removing the node itself. That might allow further reductions.\n\nSo the reduction process could involve both deletion and replacement. The objective might be to remove as many nodes as possible (or minimize total utility of kept nodes) while preserving required symbols and tree validity.\n\nGiven the limited data, we need to implement a reasonable algorithm.\n\nLet's assume we can apply two operations on a node:\n- Delete node (only if removable): removes the node and its subtree entirely. This is like cutting off the branch.\n- Replace node (only if removable): removes the node but reattaches its children to its parent (if parent exists). The node's children become children of the grandparent. This operation can only be applied if the node is not root.\n\nAfter operations, the tree must remain a valid tree (no cycles, each node has at most one parent). The root is fixed.\n\nGoal: reduce the tree such that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains.\n\nWe want to minimize some metric, maybe total utility of kept nodes? Or maximize total utility of removed nodes? The problem statement: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" \"Reduce\" likely means make it smaller. So we want to remove as many nodes as possible, but maybe we also care about utility? Utilities are positive, so removing high-utility nodes would be good if we want to minimize remaining utility. But if we want to maximize removal, we just remove as many nodes as possible regardless of utility. However, the utility might influence which nodes we choose to remove if there are trade-offs (e.g., if we have to keep one of two removable nodes to satisfy a required symbol, we might choose the one with lower utility to remove? Actually, we would want to keep the lower utility node (so we can remove the higher utility node?). Wait, if we want to reduce, we want to remove nodes. So we want to remove the ones with higher utility to maximize total removed utility? That seems arbitrary.\n\nMaybe the utility represents \"interestingness\" and we want to preserve interestingness oracle, i.e., the sum of utilities of kept nodes must exceed some threshold? Not given.\n\nGiven the lack of specifics, I think the safest approach is to simply remove all removable nodes that are not needed to preserve required symbols, using a greedy deletion (no replacement). That produces a valid reduction and is simple. The problem likely expects a straightforward solution.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that:\n- Build a lookup from node id to node dict.\n- Build a map of parent->children.\n- Determine root (parent None).\n- For each required symbol, find all node ids with that symbol.\n- For each removable node (starting from leaves or any order), check if deleting it (and its entire subtree) would cause any required symbol to have zero count in the remaining set. If not, mark it deleted and update.\n- After processing all removable nodes, collect the remaining node ids.\n- Return the list of remaining node ids (or perhaps the full tree with parent updates? But since we only delete, no parent changes). The output should be JSON-serializable. We can return {\"kept\": list_of_ids} or just the list.\n\nBut the problem doesn't specify output format. It says \"return a JSON-serializable object\". So we can choose. I'll return a dict with key \"kept\" and value list of node ids.\n\nI'll also include handling of replacement? To be safe, I might also include replacement in the algorithm. But that complicates. Since the fixture shows removable nodes that have children, and we can delete them without issue, maybe replacement is not needed for these cases. So a simple deletion-only algorithm passes.\n\nI'll implement the greedy deletion algorithm.\n\nSteps in code:\n- Parse 'case' dict: case['payload'] has 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'.\n- Build dict nodes_by_id.\n- Build children dict.\n- Build a set of removable_ids.\n- Build counts of required symbols: dict symbol->set of node ids.\n- Define function get_subtree_ids(node_id) that returns set of all ids in subtree (including node).\n- Iterate removable_ids. For each id, compute subtree set. Check if for any required symbol, the subtree set contains all occurrences of that symbol (i.e., after removal, that symbol would be lost). For each required symbol, count occurrences in subtree; if count == total count for that symbol, then we cannot delete. If deletion is safe, we mark it as to_delete, update remaining set and counts accordingly. Since we are modifying the tree, we need to recompute subtree sets after deletions? Actually, if we delete a node, its entire subtree is removed, so no further subtrees within that. For subsequent deletions, we only consider nodes that are still present. So we need to update the set of remaining nodes and recalc subtree sets for remaining nodes. We can do this by simulating: start with all nodes as remaining. For each removable node (in some order), compute its subtree within the current remaining set. If removal safe, then update remaining set by removing that subtree, and also update counts for required symbols by subtracting counts from that subtree.\n\nWe need to order the processing. If we process in arbitrary order, we might miss some deletions because a node's subtree might include another removable node that if deleted first would change counts. For example, if a required symbol appears only in a subtree of a removable node, and also in a child of that node, we might be able to delete the child first, then later delete the parent because the required symbol now appears in the child? Actually, if the child is also removable and we delete it, that removes the only occurrence? That would violate. So we need to think.\n\nBetter to treat this as a constraint: we want to find a set of nodes to delete such that the remaining set contains all required symbols. This is a set cover type problem. For each required symbol, we need to keep at least one node with that symbol. We can delete any removable node as long as its subtree doesn't contain the last remaining node for any required symbol. This can be solved by a post-order traversal: for each node, compute the set of required symbols present in its subtree. If a node is removable and its subtree does not contain the only remaining occurrences of any required symbol (i.e., the node's subtree is \"safe\" to remove), we can mark it for removal. But we need to consider that after removal, nodes above will have their subtree sets reduced. So we can do a bottom-up approach: compute for each node the set of required symbols in its subtree. Then, for each removable node, if its parent subtree (including itself?) etc. Actually, the condition for removing a node is that for every required symbol, there exists another node outside its subtree that also has that symbol. This can be checked by looking at the total counts of each symbol and the count in the node's subtree. But counts are global.\n\nWe can compute initial total counts for each required symbol. Then for each node (bottom-up), we can compute the count of each required symbol in its subtree. If the node is removable and for every required symbol, total_count - count_in_subtree > 0, then we can delete the node (and its subtree), and we must update total counts by subtracting counts_in_subtree. However, careful: if we delete a node, its children are also deleted, even if they are not removable. So we must delete the entire subtree.\n\nThus we can process nodes in any order (e.g., level order) and if a node is removable and its subtree deletion is safe, we delete it and update total counts. After deletion, the node is gone, so its ancestors' subtree counts change, but we can continue.\n\nImplementation: maintain a set of alive node ids. Compute total_counts dict initially. For each node (maybe from leaves to root), if node is removable and alive, compute subtree counts within alive nodes (only its descendants that are alive). Check if for all req sym, total_counts[sym] - subtree_counts[sym] > 0. If so, delete the subtree: remove all ids in that subtree from alive set, and subtract subtree_counts from total_counts. Continue.\n\nThis should work.\n\nWe'll use a recursive function to get subtree for a node given current alive set. Since tree size is small, recursion is fine.\n\nWe'll also need to handle the root: root is not removable, so no issue.\n\nNow, regarding replacement: if we also allow replacement, we could promote children when deleting a node. That is more complex. Given the fixture, deletion-only likely suffices.\n\nI'll write the code accordingly.\n\nOutput: I'll return a dict with \"kept\" as list of node ids kept.\n\nBut the problem says \"return a JSON-serializable object\". So we can return that dict.\n\nNow, write the solution.\n\nImplementation details:\n- def solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    nodes = payload['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(payload['required_symbols'])\n    # build node dict\n    node_by_id = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        node_by_id[n['id']] = n\n    # find root\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n            break\n    # build children adjacency\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_by_id}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    # set of alive ids\n    alive = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    # total counts for required symbols\n    total_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            total_counts[sym] = total_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # function to get subtree ids (including node) within alive set\n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\n        sub = set()\n        stack = [node_id]\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            if cur not in alive:\n                continue\n            sub.add(cur)\n            for child in children.get(cur, []):\n                if child in alive:\n                    stack.append(child)\n        return sub\n    # helper to compute subtree counts for required symbols\n    def subtree_counts(node_id):\n        sub = get_subtree_ids(node_id)\n        counts = {}\n        for sid in sub:\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\n            if sym in required_symbols:\n                counts[sym] = counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        return counts\n    # iterate: we might need multiple passes because after deletions, new nodes become leaves?\n    # We'll process all removable nodes, but after deletion, some nodes are no longer alive.\n    # We'll keep processing until no change.\n    changed = True\n    while changed:\n        changed = False\n        # iterate over a copy of removable nodes that are still alive\n        removable_alive = [nid for nid in node_by_id if node_by_id[nid]['removable'] and nid in alive]\n        for nid in removable_alive:\n            if nid not in alive:\n                continue\n            # compute subtree counts for this node\n            sc = subtree_counts(nid)\n            safe = True\n            for sym, cnt in sc.items():\n                if total_counts[sym] - cnt <= 0:\n                    safe = False\n                    break\n            if safe:\n                # delete subtree\n                sub = get_subtree_ids(nid)\n                # update counts\n                for sym, cnt in sc.items():\n                    total_counts[sym] -= cnt\n                # remove from alive\n                alive -= sub\n                changed = True\n                # break to restart iteration? or continue with modified set\n                # Since we removed nodes, the lists of removable_alive may include deleted nodes, but we check nid in alive.\n                # We can continue within the same iteration, but need to recompute subtree for each subsequent node based on updated alive set.\n                # We'll break out of inner loop and restart while loop to re-evaluate.\n                break  # restart while loop\n        # if no deletion happened in this pass, break\n    # after loop, remaining alive ids are the kept nodes\n    kept = sorted(alive)  # maybe sort for deterministic output\n    return {\"kept\": kept}\n\nBut careful: we break after first deletion to restart the while loop. This will work.\n\nWe also need to ensure we handle the case where a node's children include nodes that have been deleted; get_subtree_ids uses alive set, so it's fine.\n\nTest with first case: should delete n2, n4, n6. Output kept: root, n1, n3, n5.\n\nNow, output format: we must return JSON-serializable object. The dict {\"kept\": [...]} is fine.\n\nNow, package the Python source as a string inside JSON. Ensure escaping.\n\nWrite full implementation.\n\nAlso, note: the function must not mutate case. We are not mutating.\n\nEdge cases: if no removable nodes, just return all nodes.\n\nNow, produce final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1611,"completion_tokens":6929,"total_tokens":8540,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":6396},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":75},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}